**Scenario**: Add a 'transfer money between accounts' use case.

**Iteration 1 — simplest case**
```java
// RED
@Test
void transfer_movesMoneyBetweenAccounts() {
    Account from = new Account("A", money(100));
    Account to   = new Account("B", money(0));
    TransferService service = new TransferService();
    service.transfer(from, to, money(30));
    assertThat(from.balance()).isEqualTo(money(70));
    assertThat(to.balance()).isEqualTo(money(30));
}

// GREEN
class TransferService {
    void transfer(Account from, Account to, Money amount) {
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
    }
}
// Tests pass.
```

**Iteration 2 — insufficient funds**
```java
// RED
@Test
void transfer_throws_whenInsufficientFunds() {
    Account from = new Account("A", money(20));
    Account to   = new Account("B", money(0));
    TransferService service = new TransferService();
    assertThatThrownBy(() -> service.transfer(from, to, money(50)))
        .isInstanceOf(InsufficientFundsException.class);
    assertThat(from.balance()).isEqualTo(money(20));
    assertThat(to.balance()).isEqualTo(money(0));
}

// GREEN
class Account {
    void debit(Money amount) {
        if (balance.lessThan(amount)) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        balance = balance.minus(amount);
    }
}
```

**Iteration 3 — idempotency / atomicity**
- Add tests for: same transferId not double-applied; partial failure rolls back; concurrent transfers safe
- Drives design toward: idempotency keys, transactional boundaries, locking strategy

**Iteration 4 — wire up Spring**
- `@DataJpaTest` for repository
- `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = MOCK)` + `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` for controller
- Testcontainers + Postgres for full integration

Notice the design **emerged**: `Account` got a `debit` method with validation; `TransferService` stayed thin; persistence came later when needed. No premature `@Service` / `@Repository` ceremony — just code that the tests demanded.

**Slice tests for Spring (faster than full @SpringBootTest)**
```java
@WebMvcTest(TransferController.class)
class TransferControllerTest {
    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
    @MockBean TransferService service;

    @Test
    void transferEndpoint_returns200_onSuccess() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(post("/transfers")
                .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("{\"from\":\"A\",\"to\":\"B\",\"amount\":30}"))
           .andExpect(status().isOk());
        verify(service).transfer(any(), any(), any());
    }
}
```

**Tools**
- **JUnit 5** (parameterized tests, lifecycle, extensions)
- **AssertJ** (fluent, readable assertions)
- **Mockito** (for collaborator mocks; use sparingly)
- **Testcontainers** (real DB / Kafka / Redis in containers)
- **WireMock** (HTTP collaborator stubbing)
- **Awaitility** (async test conditions)
- **JsonAssert / KotlinX** (JSON comparison)
- **ArchUnit** (architecture tests)
- **Spock** (BDD-style tests in Groovy; readable)
